{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md && cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md'","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/SKILL.md && cat .agents/skills/code-review-pr/references/review_checklist.md'","aggregated_output":"---\nname: code-review-pr\ndescription: >-\n  Performs comprehensive, high-quality code reviews on Git pull requests, branches, commit ranges, or local working tree diffs. Analyzes code correctness, security, architecture, test coverage, and performance using automated diff analysis and structured review checklists.\n---\n\n# Code Review PR Skill\n\n## Overview\n\nThe `code-review-pr` skill provides an end-to-end framework for conducting rigorous, actionable code reviews on Git pull requests, feature branches, commit ranges, or working tree diffs. It combines automated diff scanning via `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` with structured review checklists across security, correctness, architecture, performance, and testing.\n\nUse this skill whenever asked to:\n- Review a pull request or feature branch (`git diff main...HEAD`).\n- Review recent commits (`git diff HEAD~1` or commit range).\n- Review uncommitted local changes (`git diff` or staged changes).\n- Perform a security, quality, or architecture audit of proposed code changes.\n\n---\n\n## Quick Start\n\nRun the automated PR reviewer script to generate an initial risk assessment, churn metrics, and automated smell scan:\n\n```bash\n# 1. Review branch changes against main (Default PR review)\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD\n\n# 2. Review last commit\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target HEAD~1\n\n# 3. Review uncommitted working tree changes\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target working\n\n# 4. Save review report to file\npython3 .agents/skills/code-review-pr/scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze --target main...HEAD --output review_report.md\n```\n\n---\n\n## Code Review Workflow\n\nFollow this step-by-step process when executing a code review:\n\n### Step 1: Run Automated Diff Analysis\nExecute `scripts/pr_reviewer.py analyze` for the target Git ref/range. Review the output to determine:\n- Total lines added/deleted and file churn distribution.\n- High-risk modified files (e.g., Security/Auth, Database/Schema, Infrastructure, API Contracts).\n- Test-to-source additions ratio.\n- Automated static findings (hardcoded secrets, debug leftovers, swallowed exceptions, SQL injection risks).\n\n### Step 2: Deep-Dive Code Inspection\nInspect the diffs for high-risk files using `git diff <target> -- <path>` or `view_file`. Focus on:\n1. **Logic & Edge Cases**:\n   - Boundary checks, null/nil handling, empty collections, zero division.\n   - Resource leaks (unclosed streams, file handles, DB connections).\n   - Race conditions, shared state mutation, concurrency locks.\n2. **Security & Credentials**:\n   - No hardcoded API keys, passwords, or tokens.\n   - Input validation before execution or query interpolation.\n   - Proper authorization checks on new or updated endpoints.\n3. **Architecture & Contracts**:\n   - Preserving backward compatibility on existing API functions/endpoints.\n   - Proper module separation and clean interface boundaries.\n4. **Test Coverage & Quality**:\n   - Ensuring new source logic is accompanied by unit/integration tests.\n   - Verifying tests validate actual assertions rather than just non-crashing execution.\n\n### Step 3: Reference Checklist & Severity Taxonomy\nConsult [references/review_checklist.md](references/review_checklist.md) to classify findings:\n- **P0 (Critical / Blocker)**: Security breaches, data loss risks, severe crashes, hardcoded credentials.\n- **P1 (High / Must Address)**: Logic bugs, broken API contracts, swallowed exceptions, missing critical tests.\n- **P2 (Medium / Recommended)**: Performance bottlenecks, code duplication, missing parameter validation.\n- **P3 (Low / Suggestion)**: Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs.\n\n### Step 4: Formulate Structured Review Report\nPresent findings to the user using the following template:\n\n```markdown\n# 🔍 Code Review Report\n\n## Executive Summary\n- **Target:** `<git-ref>`\n- **Verdict:** `[ APPROVED | REQUEST CHANGES | COMMENT ]`\n- **Overall Risk:** `[ LOW | MEDIUM | HIGH ]`\n- **Metrics:** `X files changed, +Y / -Z lines`\n\n## 🚨 Critical Findings (P0 / P1)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext:L123` - Short issue description.\n  ```diff\n  - problematic code\n  + suggested fix\n  ```\n\n## 💡 Improvements & Suggestions (P2 / P3)\n- **[Severity]** `path/to/file.ext` - Recommendation details.\n\n## 🧪 Test Coverage & Quality Assessment\n- Evaluation of test ratio and edge case coverage.\n\n## 🌟 Positive Highlights\n- Clean implementations, thorough documentation, or well-structured refactoring.\n```\n\n---\n\n## Utility Script Reference\n\nThe bundled helper script `scripts/pr_reviewer.py` supports the following options:\n\n| Parameter | Type | Default | Description |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| `--target` | String | `main...HEAD` | Git range/ref (`main...HEAD`, `HEAD~1`, `staged`, `working`) |\n| `--output` | String | None | File path to write report output |\n| `--format` | String | `markdown` | Output format (`markdown` or `json`) |\n| `--repo-dir` | String | Current Dir | Path to the target Git repository |\n\n---\n\n## Common Review Pitfalls\n\n1. **Nit Tunnel Vision**: Focusing heavily on formatting or naming while missing underlying logic bugs or edge-case crashes.\n2. **Ignoring Test Ratio**: Approving large source code changes without verifying corresponding test coverage additions.\n3. **Swallowing Symptoms**: Suggesting `try/except` wrappers around failing code rather than addressing root cause invalid inputs or broken state.\n# Code Review PR Checklist & Taxonomy\n\nWhen performing code reviews, evaluate changes using this structured checklist and severity taxonomy.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Finding Severity Taxonomy\n\n| Severity | Label | Definition | Examples |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **P0** | **Critical (Blocker)** | Security vulnerabilities, data loss risks, production crashes, secret leakage. Must be fixed before merging. | Hardcoded tokens, unhandled NullPointer on critical path, SQL injection, broken auth check. |\n| **P1** | **High (Must Address)** | Logic bugs, breaking API changes, missing error handling on external calls, race conditions, lack of tests for complex logic. | Swallowed exceptions, broken retry loop, missing backward compatibility in API schema. |\n| **P2** | **Medium (Recommended)** | Performance inefficiencies, code duplication, sub-optimal data structures, non-idiomatic style. | Unnecessary DB query inside loop, missing index, missing parameter validation. |\n| **P3** | **Low (Nit / Suggestion)** | Minor formatting, naming improvements, leftover comments/TODOs, minor doc polish. | Variable naming clarity, typos in docstrings, cleaning leftover TODO comments. |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Comprehensive Review Checklist\n\n### A. Logic & Correctness\n- [ ] **Edge Cases**: Does the code handle null/nil/undefined, empty collections, zero values, boundary numbers, and unexpected inputs?\n- [ ] **State & Concurrency**: Are shared state mutations thread-safe? Are locks acquired/released properly without deadlock risks?\n- [ ] **Control Flow**: Are all conditional branches covered? Do loops have guaranteed termination conditions?\n- [ ] **Error Propagation**: Are errors caught and logged/surfaced appropriately, rather than swallowed silently (`catch (Exception e) {}`)?\n\n### B. Security & Credentials\n- [ ] **No Hardcoded Secrets**: Are API keys, passwords, bearer tokens, or private keys excluded from code and config?\n- [ ] **Input Validation & Sanitization**: Are external inputs validated before processing or passing to system shells, DB queries, or HTML rendering?\n- [ ] **Authentication & Authorization**: Do endpoints verify user identity and check permissions before executing state changes?\n- [ ] **Safe Dependencies**: Are newly added packages pinned to trusted versions without known vulnerabilities?\n\n### C. Architecture & API Design\n- [ ] **Preserve API Contracts**: Are public method signatures, REST endpoints, and schema definitions backward compatible?\n- [ ] **Single Responsibility**: Are modules, classes, and functions focused on a clear, single responsibility?\n- [ ] **Coupling & Cohesion**: Is the new code properly modularized without introducing circular dependencies or leaky abstractions?\n- [ ] **Idempotence**: Are retry loops or state-changing operations safe to re-execute on transient failures?\n\n### D. Performance & Resource Efficiency\n- [ ] **N+1 Queries**: Are database or remote API calls executed in batches rather than inside loops?\n- [ ] **Resource Cleanup**: Are file handles, database connections, streams, and web sockets closed explicitly (or managed via try-with-resources / context managers)?\n- [ ] **Memory & Allocation**: Are large datasets streamed or paginated rather than loaded fully into memory at once?\n\n### E. Test Coverage & Quality\n- [ ] **Test Coverage**: Do new features or bug fixes include corresponding unit or integration tests?\n- [ ] **Test Quality**: Do tests verify actual assertions rather than just checking that code runs without throwing?\n- [ ] **Edge Case Tests**: Are failure modes, invalid inputs, and error handlers covered by unit tests?\n\n---\n\n## 3. Review Feedback Guidelines\n\n1. **Be Specific**: Always reference the exact file line number, function name, and context.\n2. **Explain the \"Why\"**: Don't just say \"change this\". Explain the risk or benefit (e.g., \"This could lead to a race condition if two threads call `transact!` simultaneously...\").\n3. **Provide Concrete Fixes**: Offer concrete, copy-pasteable diff snippets where appropriate.\n4. **Acknowledge Good Work**: Highlight well-crafted solutions, thorough tests, or clean refactorings.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff -- docs/design/dao.space.md\nrg -n 'append-only.*file|file.*reopen|daostream/gap|closed bounded result DaoStream|ds/append\"'!|:transactor.*stream|crashed writer|dead handle|checkpoint|durab|process fail|process-lifetime|unmaterialized'\"' docs/design/dao.space.md docs/design/dao.space.schema.md docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md docs/design/dao.space.index.md docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md docs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md\ngit diff --check\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"git diff -- docs/design/dao.space.md\nrg -n 'append-only.*file|file.*reopen|daostream/gap|closed bounded result DaoStream|ds/append\"'!|:transactor.*stream|crashed writer|dead handle|checkpoint|durab|process fail|process-lifetime|unmaterialized'\"' docs/design/dao.space.md docs/design/dao.space.schema.md docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md docs/design/dao.space.index.md docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md docs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md\ngit diff --check\"","aggregated_output":"diff --git a/docs/design/dao.space.md b/docs/design/dao.space.md\nindex 04f0753..e726b49 100644\n--- a/docs/design/dao.space.md\n+++ b/docs/design/dao.space.md\n@@ -57,6 +57,7 @@ admissible tuple dimension).\n - `docs/design/dao.jing.md` — the storage boundary: the content-addressed store of opaque payloads. Today this space consumes published covered-index sources from it; explicitly requested positional indexed snapshots are the future extension\n - `docs/design/dao.space.query.md` — the query library's design record: index realization, the `read-datoms` contract, and the decisions\n - `docs/design/dao.space.index.md` — the transactor-side indexing library: every agent indexes its own datoms; the covered-index realization both sides share\n+- `docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md` — the agent-side transactor value: atomic transaction records, transaction time, the local-log wiring requirement, and where durability lives\n - `docs/design/dao.stream.md` — the append-only log primitive tuples and descriptors are written through\n - `docs/design/datom.md` — tuples and datoms, content-addressed identity, the gauge/base framing\n - `docs/datomic.md` — the Datomic architecture the Transactor/Storage/Query split maps to\n@@ -178,7 +179,8 @@ semantic composition*).\n ;; count-distinct, sum, min, max, avg) with :with, predicate/function\n ;; clauses via a caller-supplied {:fns {sym fn}} option, and recursive\n ;; rules bound to % via :in (Datomic syntax; multiple bodies = OR,\n-;; terminates on cyclic data). q returns a closed bounded result DaoStream.\n+;; terminates on cyclic data). q returns a bounded result value; nothing is\n+;; opened or closed on the caller's behalf.\n ;; query/collect materializes conventional Datalog shapes.\n (query/collect\n   (query/q '[:find ?id ?task\n@@ -386,9 +388,22 @@ indexes and saves them to storage; here that duty is decentralized with the rest\n Transactor. Indexing has two stages, mirroring Datomic's memory-index → disk-index pipeline\n but without a central transactor process:\n \n-1. **Append** — the agent's writes land in its own local `dao.stream` as atomic transaction\n-   records (see `dao.space.transactor`). The local stream is the durable record; no\n-   storage handle is touched.\n+1. **Append** — the agent's writes land in its own local `dao.stream`\n+   memory-log as atomic transaction records (see\n+   [`dao.space.transactor`](dao.space.transactor.md)). The local stream is\n+   authoritative for the logical stream's process lifetime — the watermark and\n+   the published indexes are derived from it — but it is not durable: a\n+   restarted process sees a new, empty logical stream with a new identity.\n+   **The durable record is what publication puts in `dao.jing`**, and\n+   un-published writes are not durable — which was equally true before the\n+   v2 migration, when local streams were in-memory ring buffers. The\n+   pipeline mirrors Datomic's memory-index → disk-index, and a memory index\n+   is not durable either. A durable *stream* transport is not the fix: it\n+   would put a second durable record beside the content store, which is\n+   exactly the `dao.stream`/`dao.jing` unification\n+   [`dao.space.query.md`](dao.space.query.md)'s *Decisions* ruled out (\"the\n+   source stays a convention layered over the dumb content store … unifying\n+   them would undo that\"). No storage handle is touched at append.\n 2. **Publish** — when the agent runs\n    [`dao.space.index/publish-index!`](dao.space.index.md), the local stream is snapshotted,\n    the four covered indexes are built as immutable, content-addressed `dao.data.btree`\n@@ -407,22 +422,38 @@ agent-transactor loop*.\n \n ### Fault Tolerance (Crash-Only Semantics)\n \n-Because the write path uses persistent append-only `dao.stream` files, the space inherits\n-crash-only semantics natively:\n-\n-- **Data safety:** Datoms flushed before a crash are safe; append-only files have no\n-  partial-update corruption window.\n-- **Reader behavior:** A reader tailing a crashed writer's stream simply reaches the end and\n-  yields (`ds/next` returns `:blocked`); it waits for new data rather than failing.\n-- **Write recovery:** A restarted writer reopens its file in append mode; the next `ds/append!`\n-  lands safely after the last flushed datom.\n-- **Read recovery:** A reader resumes from a checkpointed cursor, so an incremental index\n-  rebuilds without reprocessing or skipping. A checkpoint persists the whole cursor map\n-  (`(:cursor result)`, not just a bare `:position` offset when the transport's cursors carry\n-  more); a cursor that has fallen behind a retention boundary returns `:daostream/gap`, and\n-  resynchronization is the caller's decision. Because `publish-index!` never rewrites the\n-  local stream — it appends to a separate intake stream — publishing cannot reorder what a\n-  local-stream cursor walks.\n+The write path is crash-only because it is built from atomic appends and\n+immutable publications — not because anything local is durable. Two failure\n+scopes behave differently, and the difference is the point:\n+\n+- **Atomicity, in both scopes:** every write is one atomic transaction\n+  record through one local append (`dao.space.transactor`), and every\n+  publication is an immutable, content-addressed manifest appended after\n+  its node blobs. A failure can leave a prefix, never a torn transaction or\n+  a partial manifest.\n+- **A writer task stops; the process lives.** Its handles remain owned and\n+  readable: a reader reaching the stopped writer's local-stream tail is\n+  told `:dao.stream/blocked` and waits for new data rather than failing —\n+  the same for a DaoJing observer over an intake stream. Retrying an\n+  interrupted publication is idempotent while the memory-log is still\n+  available: content addressing deduplicates a re-emitted prefix, and the\n+  manifest is appended last.\n+- **The process fails.** The local stream is a process-lifetime\n+  `dao.stream` memory-log and the intake streams are in-memory handles,\n+  so un-published local contents — and intake payloads a DaoJing observer\n+  has not yet materialized — may be lost; nothing tails a dead handle.\n+  Recovery has only what DaoJing has fully materialized: a restarted\n+  process sees a new, empty local logical stream with a new identity, and\n+  the agent continues from the manifest addresses it (or its\n+  configuration) names. Durability begins at publication, never before\n+  (see *The Write Path* above, and `dao.space.transactor.md`, *Where\n+  durability lives*).\n+\n+The causality-carrying checkpoint named in `dao.space.transactor.md`'s\n+*Open items* is not relief for the second scope: it is a performance\n+optimisation that would reduce the O(history) causality replay `create!`\n+pays on open, and it adds no durability — un-published history would\n+remain un-recoverable, checkpoint or not.\n \n ## Coordination: Stigmergy\n \n@@ -459,7 +490,8 @@ agent *appends a new datom* asserting the claim, and \"current state\" is a read-s\n the accreted datoms. This is the tuple space working as designed — coordination with no\n broker, no message-format negotiation, and no leader election.\n \n-The worker loop below reads with `query/q` and writes with `ds/append!` — two different\n+The worker loop below reads with `query/q` and writes with\n+`transactor/append!` — two different\n concurrency models. `append!` is a local append to the agent's own single-writer stream (one\n atomic transaction record); it is never a CAS over a shared surface. Visibility to other\n workers requires publication: `publish!` enqueues the indexes, a DaoJing observer\n@@ -473,13 +505,19 @@ implemented: the explicit `current` view masks assertions retracted for the\n same `[e a v]` via `current-state-seq` (see `dao.space.query.md`,\n *Current-state resolution*); `q`\n implements `not`/`not-join` (stratified, over the current-state-resolved index), so the\n-`(not [_ :work/claims ?w])` clause executes as written; `{:dao.stream/type :transactor :local-stream s\n-:intake-pool [...]}` is a registered `dao.stream` type (`dao.space.transactor`) whose\n-`ds/append!` deposits one atomic transaction record into the wrapper's own local stream —\n-calls through one wrapper serialize timestamp allocation and append, while each stream remains\n+`(not [_ :work/claims ?w])` clause executes as written; the write path is a\n+plain value created by `transactor/create!` over a spec\n+(`dao.space.transactor`, [`dao.space.transactor.md`](dao.space.transactor.md)) whose\n+`append!` deposits one atomic transaction record into the value's own local\n+stream — calls through one value serialize timestamp allocation and append,\n+while each stream remains\n a single-writer log with no shared write surface; and `publish!` delegates to\n `dao.space.index/publish-index!`, which snapshots the local stream and enqueues the covered\n-indexes through the intake pool. `open!` writes no registration record. The caller\n+indexes through the intake pool. The local stream must be created by\n+`dao.stream.memory-log/create!` — its declared complete retention is why\n+publication's snapshot reads from the origin; the reader/writer surface\n+checks `create!` performs do not establish that retention. `create!` writes\n+no registration record. The caller\n constructs explicit published index DaoStream descriptors and passes each as its own\n database input. The query states any union or cross-source join; no implicit\n merge can collide stream-local entity ids:\n@@ -487,7 +525,9 @@ merge can collide stream-local entity ids:\n ```clojure\n (require '[dao.jing :as jing]\n          '[dao.jing.file :as file]\n-         '[dao.stream :as ds]\n+         '[dao.stream :as stream]\n+         '[dao.stream.memory-log :as memory-log]\n+         '[dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]\n          '[dao.space.index :as index]\n          '[dao.space.query :as query]\n          '[dao.space.transactor :as transactor])\n@@ -497,23 +537,30 @@ merge can collide stream-local entity ids:\n (def store-coordinate {:dao.jing/type :dao.jing/file\n                        :path \"target/stigmergy-content.log\"})\n (def store (file/create-content-file (:path store-coordinate)))\n-(def intake (ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :ringbuffer}))\n-(def observer (jing/observer-state [intake]))\n+(def intake (:dao.stream/handle\n+             (ringbuffer/create! {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/ringbuffer\n+                                  :dao.stream.ringbuffer/capacity 65536})))\n+(def observer (jing/observer-state\n+                [{:stream intake\n+                  :cursor (:dao.stream/cursor\n+                           (stream/cursor intake :dao.stream/oldest))}]))\n \n (defn pump! []\n   (loop [obs observer]\n     (let [{:keys [state signal]} (jing/observe-step! store obs)]\n-      (when (= :ok signal) (recur state)))))\n+      (when (= :dao.stream/ok signal) (recur state)))))\n \n (defn agent-log [agent-id]\n-  (ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :transactor\n-             :local-stream (ds/open! {:dao.stream/type :ringbuffer})\n-             :intake-pool [intake]\n-             :name agent-id}))\n+  (transactor/create!\n+    {:local-stream (:dao.stream/handle\n+                     (memory-log/create!\n+                       {:dao.stream/type :dao.stream/memory-log}))\n+     :intake-pool [intake]\n+     :name agent-id}))\n \n (defn producer []\n   (let [log (agent-log \"producer\")]\n-    (ds/append! log {:db/id (random-id) :work/posted true :work/task \"process payment\"})\n+    (transactor/append! log {:db/id (random-id) :work/posted true :work/task \"process payment\"})\n     (let [{:keys [manifest-address]} (transactor/publish! log)]  ; enqueue the indexes\n       (pump!)                                                    ; materialize them\n       (index/published-index store-coordinate manifest-address)))) ; read coordinate\n@@ -530,8 +577,8 @@ merge can collide stream-local entity ids:\n                                      (not [_ :work/claims ?w])]\n                      (query/current source)))]\n         (when-let [[?w task] (first work)]\n-          (ds/append! log {:db/id (random-id) :work/claims ?w :work/by worker-id})\n-          (ds/append! log {:db/id (random-id) :work/result (process task)})\n+          (transactor/append! log {:db/id (random-id) :work/claims ?w :work/by worker-id})\n+          (transactor/append! log {:db/id (random-id) :work/result (process task)})\n           (recur))))))\n ```\n \ndocs/design/adr/0003-dao-space-is-the-event-medium.md:50:- **Volume and durability are retention questions, not admission questions.** Data is syntax\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:60:- `docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md` — the agent-side transactor value: atomic transaction records, transaction time, the local-log wiring requirement, and where durability lives\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:144:into an index, answer. It owns no durable state and never writes.\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:395:   the published indexes are derived from it — but it is not durable: a\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:397:   **The durable record is what publication puts in `dao.jing`**, and\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:398:   un-published writes are not durable — which was equally true before the\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:401:   is not durable either. A durable *stream* transport is not the 