{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md | sed -n '34,50p'\nnl -ba docs/design/dao.space.transactor.md | sed -n '164,178p'\nrg -n 'persistent append|reopens? its file|daostream/gap|dead handle|crashed writer|un-published.*recover|checkpoint.*durab|ds/append\"'!|closed bounded result|:transactor stream'\"' docs/design/dao.space*.md docs/dao.space.stigmergy.md --glob '\"'!docs/design/dao.space.v0.md'\"'\ndate -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S GMT'; TZ=Asia/Bangkok date '+%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S %z (Asia/Bangkok)'\"","aggregated_output":"    34\t- **Agents are strangers.** Two agents built by different people, running different models,\n    35\t  can coordinate only if neither needs to know the other exists. Associative matching\n    36\t  decouples in identity: a reader finds work by matching *content* (`[?w :work/posted true]`),\n    37\t  never by addressing a producer. Adding a tenth agent to a nine-agent system changes\n    38\t  nothing about the nine.\n    39\t- **Agents fail mid-action.** Append-only single-writer logs make every action an\n    40\t  attributable, atomic fact with no partial-update window — and the durable record is\n    41\t  what publication puts in `dao.jing`, not the process-lifetime local log: un-published\n    42\t  writes are not durable, and never were. An agent that stops while its process lives\n    43\t  leaves its log readable — a reader at the tail blocks rather than erroring — and\n    44\t  publication retries are idempotent; a failed process's data survives only as\n    45\t  published (crash-only semantics, `dao.space.md`, *Fault Tolerance*).\n    46\t\n    47\tThe alternative most multi-agent frameworks choose — a message bus or an orchestrator that\n    48\troutes agent-to-agent messages — re-introduces exactly the coupling the tuple space removes:\n    49\ta broker to keep alive, message formats to negotiate, recipients to name, delivery to\n    50\tguarantee. Stigmergy needs none of it. Agents read the medium, act, and deposit.\n   164\talready ruled out.\n   165\t\n   166\t## Open items\n   167\t\n   168\t- **The O(history) watermark replay.** `create!` derives `t` by reading the\n   169\t  whole retained history (`index/snapshot-datoms`), and `publish!` rebuilds\n   170\t  the indexes from the origin — both correct today *because the transport\n   171\t  cannot evict*. The eventual relief keeps one truth — the log carries its\n   172\t  own checkpoint, from which the watermark and incremental indexes resume —\n   173\t  rather than adding a second stored counter that can disagree with the\n   174\t  record. Not designed here.\n   175\t- **Schema's migration** (`dao.space.schema.md`, D10) collapses the\n   176\t  transitional mixed return — v1 `{:result :ok …}` on success, v2 outcome map\n   177\t  on refusal — to the v2 receipt under schema's own plan.\ndocs/design/dao.space.md:444:  has not yet materialized — may be lost; nothing tails a dead handle.\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:31:Concretely, a DaoSpace is **a collection of file-backed dao.streams**. `dao.space/open!` returns a **handle** to that collection; the handle plays two roles. As a **factory**, it opens member write logs — `ds/open!` delegates through the space to realize a `dao.stream.file` member, and `ds/append!` appends datom frames to it. As a **read target**, the handle answers questions over *all* member logs at once: `dao.space/q` (Datalog) and `dao.space/match` (datom pattern). The asymmetry is deliberate — **writing is streaming, reading is querying.** You never read the space *as* a stream; member streams carry datoms in, the space answers questions out.\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:51:           tuples is a catalog, opened as a handle, written by ds/append! to a member\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:92:this document the write is `(ds/append! log datom)`, where `log` is a member stream the\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:120:  (ds/append! log\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:128:  (ds/append! log\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:160:│ ds/append! frames → │  │ ds/append! frames → │           typed by dimension, strict?)\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:519:    (ds/append! log\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:536:            (ds/append! log {:db/id (random-id) ; my own local handle\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:544:              (ds/append! log {:db/id (random-id)\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:565:      (ds/append! log\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:585:            (ds/append! log\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:614:          (ds/append! log\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:649:          (ds/append! log\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:670:**`dao.stream/open!`** (through the handle) opens member write logs you `ds/append!`\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:686:### Writing — open a member log, `ds/append!` datom frames\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:698:(ds/append! log {:db/id 42 :work/status :completed :work/result \"OK\"})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:701:(ds/append! log [:db/retract work-id :work/status])\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:746:(ds/append! log {:db/id id :work/status :in-progress})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:759:(ds/append! log {:db/id id :work/status :in-progress})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:788:(ds/append! log-a {:db/id (random-id) :counter/id \"c1\" :counter 1})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:791:(ds/append! log-b {:db/id (random-id) :counter/id \"c1\" :counter 2})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:802:(ds/append! log {:db/id id :work/status :in-progress :worker agent-id})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:809:(ds/append! log {:db/id id :work/status :in-progress})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:810:(ds/append! log {:db/id id :work/assigned-to agent-id})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:814:(ds/append! log {:db/id id :work/status :in-progress :work/assigned-to agent-id})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:817:(ds/append! log\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:825:(ds/append! log {:db/id id :version 1 :work/status :in-progress})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:826:(ds/append! log {:db/id id :version 2 :work/assigned-to agent-id\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:869:;; Returns a write stream; agents ds/append! datom frames to it.\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:987:      (ds/append! log {:db/id (random-id) :work/id \"w1\" :work/status :todo})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:999:      (ds/append! a {:db/id 1025 :work/task \"task-1\"})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1000:      (ds/append! b {:db/id 1025 :work/task \"task-2\"})\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1012:      (ds/append! log {:db/id 1025 :work/id \"w1\" :work/status :todo})         ; t0\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1013:      (ds/append! log {:db/id 1025 :work/id \"w1\" :work/status :in-progress})  ; t1\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1028:      (dotimes [i 10] (ds/append! plog {:db/id (random-id) :a :work/posted :work/n i}))\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1035:          (ds/append! wlog {:db/id (random-id) :a :work/result :work/for work}))\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1167:(ds/append! log {:db/id task-id\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1187:(ds/append! log {:db/id task-id\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1192:(ds/append! log {:transaction/id tx-id\ndocs/design/dao.space.v0.md:1197:(ds/append! log {:db/id task-id :work/status :completed :timestamp (now)})\n2026-09-09 13:02:06 GMT\n2026-09-09 20:02:06 +0700 (Asia/Bangkok)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
